{
  "dom_id": "step-q350914",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/8063ba9fbe4c569519eea27e5a5cd69a8f0e708b03d153942fbf22e9345af96a.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}+2$ and $y=20-{x}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=20.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2 & =20-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/f196ef4a3b29a9d62609b0a9290f34d2cb1ef1351baa4883441fd89e9345e4ed.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=20-{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}+2.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}+2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/c006e66d82e0059b341520bebe983fa75ed144e3050ca8c4493a4b6df3e8f352.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(20)-({x}^{2}+2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[18-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=20-{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/c91a8e5ed0881ed0c8f34fa2ed6ccb5f95ff75a2f3b48e2d340f8a8856885e3a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(20)-(20-{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[18-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}((18-{x}^{2})^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}(324-36{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}(324-36{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(324x-12{x}^{3})|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\pi ((324(3)-12(3)^{3})-(324(-3)-12(-3)^{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi ((972-324)-(-972+324)) \\\\  & =\\pi (648-(-648)) \\\\  & =1296\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
